              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability associated with the `strlen` function in C++ arises when the function is used without proper validation of the input string. If `strlen` is called on a string that is not null-terminated, it can lead to undefined behavior, including buffer overflows, which can be exploited by attackers to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate input strings to ensure they are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer string handling functions or libraries that automatically handle null-termination and buffer sizes.\n3. **Static Analysis**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities in your code.\n4. **Code Review**: Regularly review code to ensure proper handling of strings and buffer sizes.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the risk associated with `strlen`, ensure that the input string is null-terminated and within a known safe buffer size. Here is an example fix:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstring>\n\n// Function to safely calculate the length of a string\nsize_t safe_strlen(const char* text, size_t max_len) {\n    if (text == nullptr) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    size_t len = 0;\n    while (len < max_len && text[len] != '\\0') {\n        ++len;\n    }\n    return len;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const char* text = \"Hello, World!\";\n    size_t max_len = 100; // Define a maximum length for safety\n    size_t length = safe_strlen(text, max_len);\n    std::cout << \"Length of the string is: \" << length << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the following standard C++ libraries:\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output stream operations.\n- `<cstring>`: For C-style string handling functions.\n\n### Relevant Links\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a640d7bf-36ff-47ea-b38a-047d65d68d45",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\n**CVE-2014-2402** is a vulnerability found in the Java Runtime Environment (JRE) component of Oracle Java SE. This vulnerability allows remote attackers to affect confidentiality, integrity, and availability via unknown vectors related to the Java Runtime Environment. It is a critical security issue that can be exploited by attackers to execute arbitrary code on the affected system.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Update Java**: Ensure that you are using the latest version of Java. Oracle regularly releases updates that patch known vulnerabilities.\n2. **Restrict Network Access**: Limit network access to systems running Java applications to trusted users and networks.\n3. **Use Security Manager**: Enable the Java Security Manager to enforce a security policy on untrusted code.\n4. **Disable Unnecessary Features**: Disable Java in web browsers if it is not required, as this reduces the attack surface.\n5. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to prevent malicious data from being processed by Java applications.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific source code fix for CVE-2014-2402 is not publicly detailed due to its nature, general best practices for securing Java applications include:\n\n- **Code Review**: Conduct thorough code reviews to identify and fix potential security issues.\n- **Use Secure Libraries**: Utilize well-maintained and secure libraries for handling sensitive operations.\n- **Exception Handling**: Implement robust exception handling to prevent application crashes and information leakage.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- **CWE-20: Improper Input Validation**: [CWE-20](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n- **CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')**: [CWE-94](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n\nPlease ensure to verify the links and resources for the most current and applicable information.\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2014-2402/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
