            {
              "id": "glog-2b447a5d-98bf-415f-bcf5-f8b7a53223c2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability associated with the `memcpy` function in C++ arises when the function is used improperly, leading to potential buffer overflow issues. The function `memcpy(out, data, size)` copies `size` bytes from the memory area pointed to by `data` to the memory area pointed to by `out`. If `size` exceeds the allocated size of `out`, it can result in a buffer overflow, which may lead to undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before performing the copy operation.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory areas are involved.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Utilize static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows in your code.\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly perform code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::min\n\nvoid safe_memcpy(char* out, const char* data, size_t out_size, size_t data_size) {\n    // Ensure that we do not copy more than the destination can hold\n    size_t size_to_copy = std::min(out_size, data_size);\n    memcpy(out, data, size_to_copy);\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for the `memcpy` function.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for the `std::min` function.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nThese links have been verified to be active and accessible to anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-41241cdb-8d3a-4638-b6f9-0989cbd0df2b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function: `memcpy`\" vulnerability arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. In C++, `memcpy` is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. If the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied, it can result in a buffer overflow, which may lead to undefined behavior, data corruption, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the specific vulnerability sink `memcpy(buffer, p, kSlopBytes)`, the issue may occur if `buffer` is not adequately sized to accommodate `kSlopBytes` bytes of data from `p`. This can lead to writing beyond the bounds of `buffer`.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Buffer Sizes**: Ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. Always validate the size of the source and destination buffers before performing memory operations.\n\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory regions are involved, as they provide better safety guarantees.\n\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Implement bounds checking to ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer.\n\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::min\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(char* buffer, size_t bufferSize, const char* p, size_t kSlopBytes) {\n    // Ensure that we do not copy more than the buffer can hold\n    size_t bytesToCopy = std::min(bufferSize, kSlopBytes);\n    memcpy(buffer, p, bytesToCopy);\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for the `memcpy` function.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for the `std::min` function to safely determine the number of bytes to copy.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8f50c567-5560-4f25-9f0a-cebbeff23028",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the use of the `memcpy` function in C/C++ programming, specifically in the line `memcpy(&buffer, p, 4)`. This line of code can lead to a buffer overflow if the destination buffer is not large enough to accommodate the data being copied. Buffer overflows can result in undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, and security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input:** Ensure that the source and destination buffers are properly validated before performing memory operations.\n2. **Use Safer Functions:** Consider using safer alternatives like `memmove` or `std::copy` in C++ which provide better safety checks.\n3. **Bounds Checking:** Always perform bounds checking to ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools:** Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the vulnerability, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough and use safer functions. Here's a revised version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::copy\n\nvoid safeCopy(char* buffer, const char* p, size_t bufferSize) {\n    if (bufferSize >= 4) {\n        std::copy(p, p + 4, buffer);\n    } else {\n        // Handle error: buffer is too small\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for `memcpy`.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for `std::copy`.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nThese resources provide further information on secure coding practices and common vulnerabilities related to buffer overflows."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-4aa85096-eeab-4f78-93d2-3b9da00ef67d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\nCVE-2013-5907 is a vulnerability found in the `pkg` component of Java, specifically affecting the Open Source Software (OSS) ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain inputs, which can lead to security issues such as unauthorized access or execution of arbitrary code. The root cause is often related to insufficient validation or sanitization of user inputs, which can be exploited by attackers to manipulate the application's behavior.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Ensure that all inputs are properly validated and sanitized. Use whitelisting techniques to allow only known good data.\n2. **Update and Patch**: Regularly update your Java environment and apply security patches provided by the maintainers.\n3. **Least Privilege**: Run applications with the least privilege necessary to limit the impact of a potential exploit.\n4. **Security Testing**: Incorporate security testing into your development lifecycle to identify and address vulnerabilities early.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo address this vulnerability, review the code handling user inputs and ensure that it includes proper validation and sanitization. For example, if the vulnerability is related to file path handling, ensure that paths are canonicalized and checked against a whitelist of allowed directories.\n\n```java\nimport java.nio.file.Paths;\nimport java.nio.file.Path;\n\npublic class SecureFileHandler {\n    private static final String BASE_DIRECTORY = \"/safe/directory/\";\n\n    public void handleFile(String userInput) {\n        Path basePath = Paths.get(BASE_DIRECTORY).toAbsolutePath().normalize();\n        Path userPath = basePath.resolve(userInput).normalize();\n\n        if (!userPath.startsWith(basePath)) {\n            throw new SecurityException(\"Invalid file path\");\n        }\n\n        // Proceed with file handling\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2013-5907/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a4ded5c9-612e-4ea6-8fef-e085c3d4f8ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-29535** affects the `tensorflow-gpu` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, which is a part of the TensorFlow library. This vulnerability arises from an issue in the implementation of certain operations that can lead to a denial of service (DoS) or potentially allow for arbitrary code execution. The root cause is typically improper validation of input data, which can lead to buffer overflows or other memory corruption issues.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. Check the TensorFlow GitHub repository or PyPI for the latest secure version.\n   \n2. **Input Validation**: Ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are properly validated. This includes checking the size, type, and format of inputs before processing.\n\n3. **Use Virtual Environments**: Isolate your Python environment using virtual environments to prevent potential exploitation from affecting other projects.\n\n4. **Regular Audits**: Regularly audit your dependencies for known vulnerabilities using tools like `pip-audit` or `safety`.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability would be implemented by the TensorFlow maintainers, a general approach to fixing such issues involves:\n\n- Adding checks to validate input dimensions and types before processing.\n- Implementing bounds checking to prevent buffer overflows.\n- Using safe memory handling functions to avoid memory corruption.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/): A list of the most critical security risks to web applications.\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/): A quick reference guide for secure coding practices.\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- **CWE-20: Improper Input Validation**: This vulnerability is closely related to CWE-20, which involves the failure to properly validate input data, leading to potential security issues.\n\nFor more detailed information, you can refer to the [CWE-20 entry](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html) on the official CWE website."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-29535/pkg:pypi/tensorflow-gpu@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ed4a2b0c-cffd-43a2-a674-ce8a3d77fc80",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as GHSA-xf83-q765-xm6m in the `tensorflow-gpu` package version 2.1.0 is a security flaw that affects the TensorFlow library, specifically its GPU-accelerated version. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain operations within the library, which can lead to potential security risks such as denial of service or arbitrary code execution. The issue is typically related to the mishandling of input data or insufficient validation of user inputs, which can be exploited by attackers to cause unexpected behavior in applications using the affected version of TensorFlow.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, users should:\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: Update to a version of TensorFlow that has addressed this vulnerability. Check the TensorFlow GitHub repository or official announcements for the latest secure version.\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are sanitized and conform to expected formats and ranges.\n3. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly check for updates and security advisories for all dependencies, including TensorFlow, to ensure that any known vulnerabilities are promptly addressed.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile specific source code fixes for vulnerabilities are typically handled by the maintainers of the library, users can contribute by:\n\n- Reviewing the TensorFlow GitHub repository for any patches or pull requests that address this issue.\n- Testing and applying any available patches to their local environment.\n- Contributing to the TensorFlow project by reporting any additional findings or improvements related to the vulnerability.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/): A list of the most critical security risks to web applications, which can provide guidance on general security practices.\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/): A quick reference guide for secure coding practices that can help prevent vulnerabilities.\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html): This CWE entry describes the general issue of improper input validation, which is often a root cause of vulnerabilities like the one described here."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "GHSA-xf83-q765-xm6m/pkg:pypi/tensorflow-gpu@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-7be3da68-e831-431e-a7c1-f4061fa6c90b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. This can occur if the source or destination buffers are not correctly sized, or if the number of bytes to copy (`kSlopBytes` in this case) exceeds the size of the destination buffer. Such vulnerabilities can lead to undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities like arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Buffer Sizes**: Ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to accommodate the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer functions like `memmove` or `std::copy` from the C++ Standard Library, which provide bounds checking.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows during development.\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly perform code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the vulnerability in the specific code snippet `memcpy(buffer_, end_, kSlopBytes)`, ensure that `buffer_` is large enough to hold `kSlopBytes` bytes. Here is a safer version using `std::copy`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm> // for std::copy\n#include <cstring>   // for std::memcpy\n\n// Assuming buffer_ and end_ are pointers to the start and end of the buffer\n// and kSlopBytes is the number of bytes to copy\n\n// Ensure buffer_ is large enough\nif (buffer_ + kSlopBytes <= end_) {\n    std::copy(buffer_, buffer_ + kSlopBytes, end_);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer is not large enough\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- `<algorithm>`: For `std::copy`\n- `<cstring>`: For `std::memcpy` (if still needed elsewhere in the code)\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-0b7910e1-19b1-4176-9069-f3d1391287bd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. This occurs when the size of the data being copied (`s`) exceeds the size of the destination buffer (`ptr`). Such vulnerabilities can lead to undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, and security breaches such as arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input Sizes**: Always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer functions like `memmove` or C++ standard library functions that perform bounds checking.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Utilize static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows during development.\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly perform code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <iostream> // Required for std::cout\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(char* dest, const char* src, size_t destSize, size_t dataSize) {\n    if (dataSize > destSize) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: Source data size exceeds destination buffer size.\" << std::endl;\n        return;\n    }\n    memcpy(dest, src, dataSize);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const size_t bufferSize = 10;\n    char buffer[bufferSize];\n    const char* data = \"Hello\";\n    size_t dataSize = strlen(data) + 1; // +1 for null terminator\n\n    safeMemcpy(buffer, data, bufferSize, dataSize);\n\n    std::cout << \"Buffer content: \" << buffer << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for `memcpy`.\n- `<iostream>`: Required for `std::cout` and `std::cerr`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-013b1f6e-c552-4706-9c3c-44af00d7baf0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability CVE-2020-15206 affects the `tensorflow` package, specifically version 2.1.0, available on PyPI. This vulnerability arises from a flaw in the implementation of certain TensorFlow operations that can lead to a denial of service (DoS) or potentially allow for arbitrary code execution. The issue is due to improper validation of user-supplied data, which can result in out-of-bounds reads or writes.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: Update to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. The TensorFlow team has addressed this issue in later releases, so upgrading to the latest stable version is advised.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are within expected bounds and types.\n\n3. **Use Virtual Environments**: Run TensorFlow within isolated environments to limit the impact of any potential exploitation.\n\n4. **Monitor and Audit**: Regularly monitor and audit your systems for any unusual activity that could indicate exploitation attempts.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TensorFlow team has addressed this issue in subsequent releases. The fix involves adding proper checks and validations to ensure that inputs are within the expected range and type. Reviewing the TensorFlow GitHub repository for the specific commit that addresses this issue can provide insights into the exact changes made.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to the following CWE:\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nThese resources provide further guidance on secure coding practices and how to protect against similar vulnerabilities."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2020-15206/pkg:pypi/tensorflow@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-7bf0292a-6d37-49e2-b409-c54d83f5cd44",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\nCVE-2013-0422 is a critical security vulnerability in the Java Runtime Environment (JRE) that allows remote attackers to execute arbitrary code on vulnerable installations. This vulnerability is due to a flaw in the Java security model that fails to properly restrict the privileges of Java applets. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by convincing a user to visit a specially crafted web page that contains a malicious Java applet.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Update Java**: Ensure that you are using the latest version of Java, as Oracle has released patches to address this vulnerability.\n2. **Disable Java in Web Browsers**: If Java is not required for web applications, disable it in your web browsers to prevent exploitation through malicious applets.\n3. **Use Security Software**: Employ security software that can detect and block malicious Java applets.\n4. **Restrict Java Execution**: Configure your system to only allow Java applets from trusted sources.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nSince this vulnerability is within the Java Runtime Environment itself, the fix involves updating to a patched version of Java rather than modifying application source code. Ensure that your Java environment is updated to a version that includes the fix for CVE-2013-0422.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-284: Improper Access Control](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/284.html)\n\nPlease ensure that you regularly check for updates and patches for your software to protect against known vulnerabilities.\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2013-0422/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a57d0f19-f3aa-482b-b5f6-d3618bbac992",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function: `memcpy`\" vulnerability arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. In C++, `memcpy` is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. If the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied, it can result in a buffer overflow, which may lead to undefined behavior, data corruption, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the specific vulnerability sink `memcpy(buffer, p, kSlopBytes)`, the issue may occur if `buffer` is not adequately sized to accommodate `kSlopBytes` bytes of data from `p`. This can lead to writing beyond the bounds of `buffer`.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Buffer Sizes**: Ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. Always validate the size of the source and destination buffers before performing memory operations.\n\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory regions are involved, as they provide better safety guarantees.\n\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Implement bounds checking to ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer.\n\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::min\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(char* buffer, size_t bufferSize, const char* p, size_t kSlopBytes) {\n    // Ensure that we do not copy more than the buffer can hold\n    size_t bytesToCopy = std::min(bufferSize, kSlopBytes);\n    memcpy(buffer, p, bytesToCopy);\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for the `memcpy` function.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for the `std::min` function to safely determine the number of bytes to copy.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ba6c59b2-b6a4-4589-90cb-4d72b30b1b9c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. This occurs when the size of the data being copied exceeds the size of the destination buffer, which can result in memory corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input Sizes**: Always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer functions like `memmove` or `std::copy` from the C++ Standard Library, which provide better safety checks.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Utilize static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows during development.\n4. **Boundary Checks**: Implement boundary checks to ensure that buffer sizes are respected.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <iostream> // Required for std::cout\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(char* dest, const char* src, size_t destSize, size_t dataSize) {\n    if (dataSize > destSize) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: Source data size exceeds destination buffer size.\" << std::endl;\n        return;\n    }\n    memcpy(dest, src, dataSize);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const size_t bufferSize = 10;\n    char buffer[bufferSize];\n    const char* data = \"Hello\";\n    size_t dataSize = strlen(data) + 1; // +1 for null terminator\n\n    safeMemcpy(buffer, data, bufferSize, dataSize);\n\n    std::cout << \"Buffer content: \" << buffer << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for `memcpy`.\n- `<iostream>`: Required for `std::cout` and `std::cerr`.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
