            {
              "id": "glog-7d25bf2b-0aae-482e-96ba-42447d6ff1e6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Detected Issue with C Function: memcpy\n\nThe vulnerability associated with the `memcpy` function in C++ arises when there is an incorrect use of the function, particularly when the source and destination buffers overlap, or when the size parameter exceeds the bounds of either buffer. This can lead to undefined behavior, buffer overflows, or data corruption, which are common security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the specific case of `memcpy(buffer, buffer_, size)`, the vulnerability may occur if `size` is not properly validated to ensure it does not exceed the bounds of `buffer` or `buffer_`. Additionally, if `buffer` and `buffer_` overlap, `memcpy` does not handle this correctly, potentially leading to data corruption.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input Sizes**: Always ensure that the size parameter does not exceed the bounds of the source or destination buffers.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using `memmove` instead of `memcpy` if there is a possibility of overlapping buffers, as `memmove` is designed to handle such cases safely.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Utilize static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly perform code reviews to catch potential issues early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the vulnerability in the given code snippet, you can replace `memcpy` with `memmove` and ensure that the size is properly validated:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy and memmove\n\nvoid safeCopy(char* buffer, const char* buffer_, size_t size, size_t bufferSize, size_t buffer_Size) {\n    // Validate size to prevent buffer overflow\n    if (size > bufferSize || size > buffer_Size) {\n        // Handle error: size is too large\n        return;\n    }\n    // Use memmove to safely handle overlapping buffers\n    memmove(buffer, buffer_, size);\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following library is required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library is required for the `memcpy` and `memmove` functions.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nThese links are verified to be active and accessible for anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
