            {
              "id": "glog-ee11885a-9b91-4fde-a0e3-f746e0b2ed76",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows or memory corruption. This can occur if the source or destination buffers are not correctly sized or if the size parameter is incorrect. In the provided code snippet, the vulnerability is due to the use of `memcpy` with potentially incorrect size calculations or buffer boundaries, which can lead to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Buffer Sizes**: Ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer functions like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory areas are possible.\n3. **Boundary Checks**: Always perform boundary checks before copying data to ensure that the source and destination buffers are within valid ranges.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other memory issues.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability in the code snippet, ensure that the size of the data being copied is correctly calculated and that the destination buffer is adequately sized:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::copy\n\n// Assuming elem and reader are defined and initialized properly\n// Fix: Ensure that the size is correctly calculated and buffers are valid\nstd::copy((reader).ptr, (reader).ptr + sizeof(elem), reinterpret_cast<char*>(&elem));\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For using `memcpy` or other C-style memory functions.\n- `<algorithm>`: For using `std::copy`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration - CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-bededf7d-5831-44a6-a447-d04cda769304",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the use of the `memcpy` function in C++. The function call `memcpy(&(elem), (reader).ptr, sizeof((elem)))` can lead to several issues if not handled properly. The primary concern is that `memcpy` does not perform bounds checking, which can result in buffer overflows if the source or destination buffers are not correctly sized. This can lead to undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the source and destination buffers are large enough to accommodate the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory regions are involved.\n3. **Input Validation**: Validate all inputs to ensure they are within expected ranges and sizes.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the vulnerability, ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. Here is a revised version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::min\n\n// Assuming elem and reader are defined elsewhere\n// Ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of elem\nsize_t elemSize = sizeof(elem);\nsize_t readerSize = /* size of data pointed by (reader).ptr */;\nsize_t copySize = std::min(elemSize, readerSize);\n\nmemcpy(&(elem), (reader).ptr, copySize);\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nThe following libraries are required for the code example to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for the `memcpy` function.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for the `std::min` function.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-60701f97-6492-41ac-857a-6d332716b3ab",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used incorrectly, potentially leading to buffer overflows or memory corruption. The function `memcpy(&dest, &from, sizeof(dest))` is problematic because it uses the size of the destination buffer, which may not accurately reflect the size of the data being copied. This can result in copying more data than the destination can hold, leading to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Proper Buffer Sizes**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer functions like `memmove` or `std::copy` in C++ which provide better safety guarantees.\n3. **Boundary Checks**: Implement boundary checks to ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other memory issues in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability, ensure that the size of the data being copied is used instead of the size of the destination buffer. Here is a corrected version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(void* dest, const void* from, size_t dataSize) {\n    memcpy(dest, from, dataSize);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    char dest[10];\n    char from[10] = \"123456789\";\n    \n    // Correct usage: ensure the size of the data being copied is used\n    safeMemcpy(dest, from, sizeof(from));\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for using the `memcpy` function.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-d558a666-087b-407d-9fae-8c01545c9533",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\n**CVE-2019-2894** is a vulnerability found in the Oracle WebLogic Server component of Oracle Fusion Middleware. It affects the Java SE component and is related to the deserialization of untrusted data. This vulnerability allows unauthenticated attackers with network access via T3 to compromise the Oracle WebLogic Server. Successful attacks of this vulnerability can result in unauthorized ability to cause a hang or frequently repeatable crash (complete DOS) of Oracle WebLogic Server.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Apply Patches**: Ensure that you have applied the latest patches provided by Oracle for WebLogic Server. Regularly check for updates and apply them promptly.\n\n2. **Network Segmentation**: Limit network access to the WebLogic Server. Use firewalls and network segmentation to restrict access to trusted sources only.\n\n3. **Deserialization Controls**: Implement controls to prevent deserialization of untrusted data. Consider using libraries that provide safer deserialization mechanisms or validate the data before deserialization.\n\n4. **Monitoring and Logging**: Enable detailed logging and monitoring to detect any suspicious activities or attempts to exploit the vulnerability.\n\n5. **Security Configuration**: Review and harden the security configuration of your WebLogic Server. Disable unnecessary services and ports.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the vulnerability is primarily related to configuration and patching, developers should ensure that any custom code does not perform deserialization of untrusted data. Consider using the following practices:\n\n- Use a safe deserialization library that enforces type checks.\n- Validate and sanitize all input data before deserialization.\n- Implement a whitelist of acceptable classes for deserialization.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Deserialization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten 2021 - A08:2021 Software and Data Integrity Failures](https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2019-2894/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-c1278523-90be-4157-949b-0e69ecaab4c9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\nCVE-2019-2842 is a vulnerability found in the Java SE component of Oracle Java SE (subcomponent: Libraries). It affects versions prior to 7u221, 8u211, 11.0.3, and 12.0.1. This vulnerability allows an unauthenticated attacker with network access via multiple protocols to compromise Java SE. Successful attacks of this vulnerability can result in unauthorized ability to cause a partial denial of service (partial DOS) of Java SE.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n1. **Update Java SE**: Ensure that you are using the latest version of Java SE. Oracle has released patches for this vulnerability, so updating to the latest version will mitigate the risk.\n2. **Network Segmentation**: Limit network access to Java SE components by implementing network segmentation and access controls.\n3. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to prevent malformed data from being processed by the application.\n4. **Monitoring and Logging**: Enable detailed logging and monitoring to detect any unusual activity that might indicate an exploitation attempt.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability is not publicly available, the general recommendation is to apply the latest security patches provided by Oracle. Regularly review and update your codebase to ensure it adheres to secure coding practices.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nPlease ensure that you regularly check for updates and patches from Oracle and apply them promptly to maintain the security of your systems.\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2019-2842/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-dc8d6008-2634-4a78-a0c4-e3380ede410c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows, data corruption, or undefined behavior. This vulnerability typically occurs when the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied, or when the source and destination overlap, which is not allowed by `memcpy`.\n\nIn the specific example `memcpy(p, &v, sizeof v)`, the vulnerability may occur if the buffer `p` is not large enough to accommodate the size of `v`. This can lead to overwriting adjacent memory, causing unpredictable behavior or security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Buffer Size**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `memmove` if there is a possibility of overlapping memory regions, or `std::copy` in C++ for type-safe copying.\n3. **Boundary Checks**: Implement boundary checks to prevent buffer overflows.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other memory issues.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability sink, ensure that the destination buffer `p` is properly sized and consider using safer alternatives:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::copy\n\n// Example fix using std::copy\nvoid safeCopy(void* p, const void* v, size_t size) {\n    // Ensure p is large enough to hold the data\n    if (p == nullptr || v == nullptr) return; // Check for null pointers\n\n    // Use std::copy for type-safe copying\n    std::copy(static_cast<const char*>(v), static_cast<const char*>(v) + size, static_cast<char*>(p));\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for `memcpy`.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for `std::copy`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nThese links are verified to be active and accessible for anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-dc760bbe-d893-4f14-ae11-1a937ae95a77",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2022-35959** affects the `tensorflow-gpu` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, which is a part of the TensorFlow library used for machine learning tasks. This vulnerability arises from improper validation of user-supplied input, which can lead to potential security risks such as denial of service or arbitrary code execution. The issue is rooted in the way certain operations handle input data, allowing attackers to exploit this flaw by providing crafted inputs that can cause the application to crash or execute unintended operations.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. Check the TensorFlow GitHub repository or PyPI for the latest secure version.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all inputs are sanitized and conform to expected formats and types before processing.\n\n3. **Use Security Tools**: Employ static analysis tools and dependency checkers to identify and remediate vulnerabilities in your codebase and dependencies.\n\n4. **Monitor and Patch**: Regularly monitor security advisories for updates on vulnerabilities and apply patches promptly.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo address this vulnerability, developers should ensure that all input data is validated before being processed by TensorFlow operations. This can be done by adding checks to confirm that inputs are within expected bounds and types. Additionally, consider using TensorFlow's built-in functions that provide safer handling of inputs.\n\n```python\nimport tensorflow as tf\n\ndef safe_tensor_operation(input_tensor):\n    # Example of input validation\n    if not isinstance(input_tensor, tf.Tensor):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a TensorFlow tensor.\")\n    \n    # Further processing with validated input\n    result = tf.math.reduce_sum(input_tensor)\n    return result\n\n# Example usage\ntry:\n    input_data = tf.constant([1, 2, 3])\n    output = safe_tensor_operation(input_data)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(f\"Input validation error: {e}\")\n```\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to **CWE-20: Improper Input Validation**. More information can be found on the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html).\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-35959/pkg:pypi/tensorflow-gpu@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8862fd8a-3a98-4cab-b676-1bf582fd8913",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\n**CVE-2017-3514** is a vulnerability found in the Java SE component of Oracle Java SE (subcomponent: Libraries). It affects versions prior to 7u131, 8u121, and 9. This vulnerability allows an unauthenticated attacker to cause a denial of service (DoS) by leveraging the improper handling of certain inputs. The issue is related to the deserialization of untrusted data, which can lead to application crashes or other unintended behaviors.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Update Java SE**: Ensure that you are using the latest version of Java SE. Oracle has released patches for this vulnerability, so upgrading to a version that is 7u131, 8u121, 9, or later will mitigate the issue.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only trusted data is deserialized. Avoid deserializing data from untrusted sources.\n\n3. **Use Security Manager**: Consider using Java's Security Manager to enforce a security policy that restricts the actions that can be performed by untrusted code.\n\n4. **Deserialization Filters**: Use deserialization filters to control what classes can be deserialized. This can prevent the deserialization of potentially harmful classes.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability in your code, you can implement a deserialization filter. Here is an example of how to set a global deserialization filter in Java:\n\n```java\nimport java.io.ObjectInputFilter;\n\npublic class DeserializationFilterExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ObjectInputFilter filter = ObjectInputFilter.Config.createFilter(\"maxdepth=5;!*\");\n        ObjectInputFilter.Config.setSerialFilter(filter);\n\n        // Your deserialization logic here\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis filter limits the depth of the object graph to 5 and blocks all other classes (`!*`). Adjust the filter rules according to your application's needs.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Deserialization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten 2021: A08:2021 - Software and Data Integrity Failures](https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2017-3514/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-5c278a6e-db54-4fa5-8a80-feb4922bf944",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows, data corruption, or undefined behavior. This vulnerability typically occurs when the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied, or when the source and destination overlap, which is not allowed by `memcpy`.\n\nIn the specific example `memcpy(p, &v, sizeof v)`, the vulnerability may occur if the buffer `p` is not large enough to accommodate the size of `v`. This can lead to overwriting adjacent memory, causing unpredictable behavior or security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Buffer Size**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `memmove` if there is a possibility of overlapping memory regions, or `std::copy` in C++ for type-safe copying.\n3. **Boundary Checks**: Implement boundary checks to prevent buffer overflows.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other memory issues.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability sink, ensure that the destination buffer `p` is properly sized and consider using safer alternatives:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::copy\n\n// Example fix using std::copy\nvoid safeCopy(void* p, const void* v, size_t size) {\n    // Ensure p is large enough to hold the data\n    if (p == nullptr || v == nullptr) return; // Check for null pointers\n\n    // Use std::copy for type-safe copying\n    std::copy(static_cast<const char*>(v), static_cast<const char*>(v) + size, static_cast<char*>(p));\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for `memcpy`.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for `std::copy`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nThese links are verified to be active and accessible for anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
