              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. This occurs when the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied, which can result in memory corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the specific code snippet `memcpy(outbuf_ + size_, bytes, n)`, the vulnerability may occur if the size of `outbuf_` is not properly checked to ensure it can accommodate `n` bytes starting from the offset `size_`. If `outbuf_` is smaller than `size_ + n`, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the buffer before performing the copy operation.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `memmove` or `std::copy` from the C++ Standard Library, which provide additional safety checks.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly perform code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the vulnerability in the given code snippet, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to accommodate the data being copied:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <stdexcept> // Required for std::runtime_error\n\nvoid safe_memcpy(char* outbuf_, size_t outbuf_size, size_t size_, const char* bytes, size_t n) {\n    if (size_ + n > outbuf_size) {\n        throw std::runtime_error(\"Buffer overflow detected\");\n    }\n    memcpy(outbuf_ + size_, bytes, n);\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for the `memcpy` function.\n- `<stdexcept>`: Required for `std::runtime_error` to handle exceptions.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2525bfab-0049-4454-9526-6a6c77dc320d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\nCVE-2013-5850 is a vulnerability found in the `pkg` component of Java, specifically affecting the Open Source Software (OSS) ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain inputs, which can lead to a security bypass or unauthorized access. The issue is primarily due to insufficient validation of user-supplied data, which can be exploited by attackers to execute arbitrary code or perform unauthorized actions within the application.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n1. **Input Validation**: Ensure that all user inputs are properly validated and sanitized. Implement strict input validation rules to prevent malicious data from being processed.\n2. **Access Controls**: Implement robust access control mechanisms to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive functionalities.\n3. **Regular Updates**: Keep your Java environment and all related packages up to date with the latest security patches and updates.\n4. **Security Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and fix potential vulnerabilities in the codebase.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\nTo address this vulnerability, consider the following code fix recommendations:\n\n- **Sanitize Inputs**: Use libraries or frameworks that provide input sanitization features to clean user inputs before processing.\n- **Use Security Libraries**: Leverage security libraries that offer built-in protection against common vulnerabilities.\n- **Implement Error Handling**: Ensure that your application has proper error handling mechanisms to prevent leakage of sensitive information.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2013-5850/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-f394d684-3c14-4ebd-a03c-199c9abe776c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. This can occur if the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied, which can result in undefined behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(m_backFrame.get()->data, buffer, 4 * height * width);\n```\n\nThe vulnerability may occur if `m_backFrame.get()->data` does not have sufficient space to accommodate `4 * height * width` bytes. This can lead to overwriting adjacent memory, causing the aforementioned issues.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Buffer Size**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory regions are involved.\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Implement bounds checking to verify that the source and destination buffers are appropriately sized.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows during development.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the vulnerability, ensure that the destination buffer is adequately sized before performing the `memcpy` operation. Here's a revised version of the code with a check:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::min\n\n// Assuming m_backFrame.get()->dataSize is the size of the destination buffer\nsize_t dataSize = m_backFrame.get()->dataSize;\nsize_t copySize = 4 * height * width;\n\n// Ensure we do not copy more than the destination can hold\nsize_t safeCopySize = std::min(dataSize, copySize);\n\nmemcpy(m_backFrame.get()->data, buffer, safeCopySize);\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For the `memcpy` function.\n- `<algorithm>`: For the `std::min` function.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-51bc3e68-8365-4f90-8d23-be97d173c0a4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows. This occurs when the size of the data being copied (`n`) exceeds the size of the destination buffer (`dest_`). Such vulnerabilities can lead to undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, and security vulnerabilities like arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input Sizes**: Always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer functions like `memmove` or `std::copy` from the C++ Standard Library, which provide better safety checks.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows in your code.\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly conduct code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the vulnerability in the specific code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::min\n\nvoid safe_memcpy(char* dest_, const char* data, size_t dest_size, size_t n) {\n    // Ensure we do not copy more than the destination can hold\n    size_t copy_size = std::min(dest_size, n);\n    memcpy(dest_, data, copy_size);\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For the `memcpy` function.\n- `<algorithm>`: For the `std::min` function.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ba80758b-5bad-4f22-a3f9-9d4ff699bc45",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability associated with the `strlen` function in C++ arises from its inability to handle non-null-terminated strings. The `strlen` function calculates the length of a string by searching for the null terminator (`'\\0'`). If the string is not properly null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory beyond the intended buffer, potentially leading to undefined behavior, memory corruption, or security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Proper Null-Termination**: Always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer string handling functions or classes, such as `std::string` in C++, which manage null-termination automatically.\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Implement bounds checking to ensure that operations on strings do not exceed the allocated buffer size.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstring> // Required for strlen\n\nvoid safe_strlen(const char* c_str, size_t buffer_size) {\n    if (c_str == nullptr) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: Null pointer provided.\" << std::endl;\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Ensure the string is null-terminated within the buffer size\n    size_t length = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < buffer_size; ++i) {\n        if (c_str[i] == '\\0') {\n            length = i;\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (length == 0 && c_str[buffer_size - 1] != '\\0') {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: String is not null-terminated within the buffer size.\" << std::endl;\n        return;\n    }\n\n    std::cout << \"String length is: \" << length << std::endl;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const char buffer[10] = \"Hello\";\n    safe_strlen(buffer, sizeof(buffer));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<iostream>`: Required for input and output operations.\n- `<cstring>`: Required for using `strlen` and other C-style string functions.\n\n### Relevant Links\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-32d727a3-dc99-42f2-9451-dfeeb0379258",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\n**CVE-2012-5068** is a vulnerability found in the Java package management system, `pkg`, which is part of the OpenSolaris operating system. This vulnerability arises due to improper handling of package metadata, which can lead to arbitrary code execution. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by crafting a malicious package that, when processed by the `pkg` system, executes arbitrary code with the privileges of the user running the `pkg` command.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Update and Patch**: Ensure that your system is updated with the latest security patches provided by the vendor. Regularly check for updates to the `pkg` system and apply them promptly.\n\n2. **Least Privilege Principle**: Run the `pkg` command with the least privileges necessary. Avoid running package management commands as the root user unless absolutely necessary.\n\n3. **Validate Packages**: Only install packages from trusted sources. Verify the integrity and authenticity of packages before installation.\n\n4. **Monitor and Audit**: Regularly monitor and audit system logs for any suspicious activity related to package management operations.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo address this vulnerability, developers should ensure that the `pkg` system properly validates and sanitizes all package metadata before processing. This includes:\n\n- Implementing strict input validation to ensure that metadata does not contain any malicious code.\n- Using secure coding practices to prevent buffer overflows and other common vulnerabilities.\n- Conducting thorough code reviews and security testing to identify and fix potential security issues.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to the following CWE:\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nPlease ensure to verify the links for accessibility and validity.\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2012-5068/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-205d2514-707b-4592-bfa3-d72bc84d806c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\n**Vulnerability CVE-2025-26597** in the `libX11` package is a critical security flaw that affects applications using the Open Source Software (OSS) programming language. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation in the `libX11` library, which can lead to buffer overflow conditions. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code on the affected system, potentially leading to a full system compromise.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Update the Library**: Ensure that you are using the latest version of `libX11`. The maintainers of the library may have released a patch to address this vulnerability.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all inputs are sanitized and validated before processing.\n\n3. **Use Compiler Security Features**: Enable security features provided by modern compilers, such as stack canaries, ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization), and DEP (Data Execution Prevention).\n\n4. **Regular Security Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and mitigate potential vulnerabilities.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo address the buffer overflow issue, ensure that all buffer operations in the `libX11` codebase are performed with proper bounds checking. For example, replace vulnerable functions like `strcpy` with safer alternatives like `strncpy` or `snprintf`, which allow you to specify the maximum number of bytes to copy.\n\n```c\n// Example of replacing strcpy with strncpy\nchar destination[BUFFER_SIZE];\nstrncpy(destination, source, BUFFER_SIZE - 1);\ndestination[BUFFER_SIZE - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2025-26597/suse:libX11:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-11b238b2-57b6-4330-81a0-19bae841b796",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\nCVE-2012-5069 is a vulnerability found in the Java Runtime Environment (JRE) component of Oracle Java SE. This vulnerability allows remote attackers to affect confidentiality, integrity, and availability via unknown vectors related to the Libraries component. The issue arises from improper handling of certain data, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service (DoS) condition.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Update Java**: Ensure that you are using the latest version of Java, as Oracle regularly releases patches that address known vulnerabilities.\n2. **Restrict Permissions**: Limit the permissions of Java applications to the minimum necessary for their operation.\n3. **Network Segmentation**: Isolate systems running Java applications from critical network resources to minimize potential impact.\n4. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to prevent malicious data from being processed by Java applications.\n5. **Security Tools**: Use security tools and firewalls to monitor and block suspicious activities related to Java applications.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific source code fix for CVE-2012-5069 is not publicly detailed due to its nature as a proprietary vulnerability, general best practices for preventing similar vulnerabilities include:\n\n- **Sanitize Inputs**: Ensure all inputs are sanitized and validated before processing.\n- **Use Secure Libraries**: Utilize well-maintained and secure libraries for handling data.\n- **Code Review**: Conduct regular code reviews to identify and fix potential security issues.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2012-5069/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8704ebb8-4a40-4015-b608-fd582d5c98bb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-29589** affects the `tensorflow` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, available on PyPI. This vulnerability arises from a flaw in the implementation of the `tf.raw_ops.SparseCountSparseOutput` operation. The issue is due to a missing validation of the input arguments, which can lead to a heap buffer overflow. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service (DoS) by crashing the application.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: Update to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. The TensorFlow team has addressed this issue in later releases, so upgrading to the latest stable version is advised.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement additional input validation checks in your application to ensure that inputs to TensorFlow operations are within expected bounds and types.\n\n3. **Security Patches**: Regularly apply security patches and updates to your dependencies to protect against known vulnerabilities.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe fix for this vulnerability involves adding proper validation checks for the input arguments to the `tf.raw_ops.SparseCountSparseOutput` operation. Here is a conceptual example of how such a fix might look:\n\n```python\ndef safe_sparse_count_sparse_output(input_tensor, indices, dense_shape):\n    # Validate input_tensor\n    if not isinstance(input_tensor, tf.Tensor):\n        raise ValueError(\"input_tensor must be a TensorFlow tensor\")\n\n    # Validate indices\n    if not isinstance(indices, tf.Tensor) or indices.dtype != tf.int64:\n        raise ValueError(\"indices must be a TensorFlow tensor of type int64\")\n\n    # Validate dense_shape\n    if not isinstance(dense_shape, tf.Tensor) or dense_shape.dtype != tf.int64:\n        raise ValueError(\"dense_shape must be a TensorFlow tensor of type int64\")\n\n    # Proceed with the operation if all checks pass\n    return tf.raw_ops.SparseCountSparseOutput(\n        input_tensor=input_tensor,\n        indices=indices,\n        dense_shape=dense_shape\n    )\n```\n\n### Relevant Links\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to **CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write**. More information can be found on the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-29589/pkg:pypi/tensorflow@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-5db0db24-9474-468b-9653-37c71e983513",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2022-21739** affects the `tensorflow` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, available on PyPI. This vulnerability arises from an issue in the `SparseCountSparseOutput` operation, where the implementation does not properly validate the input arguments. This can lead to a heap buffer overflow, potentially allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service (DoS) by crashing the application.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade TensorFlow to a version where this vulnerability is patched. Check the TensorFlow release notes for the version where this issue is resolved and update accordingly.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are validated before being processed. This can help prevent malformed inputs from triggering vulnerabilities.\n\n3. **Use Virtual Environments**: Run TensorFlow applications in isolated environments to limit the impact of a potential exploit.\n\n4. **Regular Security Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and mitigate potential vulnerabilities in your codebase.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe fix for this vulnerability involves adding proper validation checks for the input arguments in the `SparseCountSparseOutput` operation. Here is a conceptual example of how input validation might be implemented:\n\n```python\ndef sparse_count_sparse_output(input_tensor):\n    # Example validation check\n    if not isinstance(input_tensor, tf.SparseTensor):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a SparseTensor\")\n\n    # Additional validation logic as needed\n    # ...\n\n    # Proceed with the operation\n    # ...\n```\n\nEnsure that similar validation logic is applied to all relevant operations in your TensorFlow code.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\nThese resources provide additional guidance on secure coding practices and understanding common vulnerabilities."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-21739/pkg:pypi/tensorflow@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ba626d8b-bd94-455c-90eb-595bee9fc03c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the use of the `memcpy` function in C++. The `memcpy` function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. However, if not used carefully, it can lead to buffer overflows, which can cause undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as data corruption or unauthorized access to sensitive data.\n\nIn the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(mappedData + (i*mappedResource.RowPitch), buffer + (i*nRowSpan), nRowSpan);\n```\n\nThe potential issue is that if `nRowSpan` exceeds the size of the destination buffer or the source buffer, it can lead to a buffer overflow. This can happen if the size of the data being copied is not properly validated against the size of the destination buffer.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input Sizes**: Always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` which provide better safety checks.\n3. **Boundary Checks**: Implement boundary checks to ensure that the indices and sizes used in memory operations are within valid ranges.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow in the `memcpy` usage, you can add boundary checks before performing the copy operation:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t destinationSize = /* size of the destination buffer */;\nsize_t sourceSize = /* size of the source buffer */;\n\nif ((i * mappedResource.RowPitch + nRowSpan <= destinationSize) &&\n    (i * nRowSpan + nRowSpan <= sourceSize)) {\n    memcpy(mappedData + (i * mappedResource.RowPitch), buffer + (i * nRowSpan), nRowSpan);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: sizes are not valid\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not require any additional library dependencies beyond the standard C++ library. However, if you are using specific data structures or types, ensure that the appropriate headers are included.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
